<% content_for :page_title, "Sign-in help" %>
<% content_for :description, "Why Mastodon asks you to authorize Waterhole again, and what can stop a sign-in." %>

<%
  section_class = "scroll-mt-6 rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 bg-white dark:bg-stone-900 p-5"
  heading_class = "mb-3 font-semibold"
  body_class = "space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300"
  link_class = "font-medium underline"
%>

<h1 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Sign-in help</h1>

<p class="mb-6 text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">
  Every time a moderator signs in, Waterhole checks that the Mastodon server
  answering for your instance's domain is still the one that signed up here.
  A domain can change hands, and whoever runs it next must not see your
  team's applicants and notes. That check is behind everything on this page.
</p>

<div class="space-y-6">
  <section id="authorize-again" class="<%= section_class %>">
    <h2 class="<%= heading_class %>">Mastodon asks me to authorize Waterhole again</h2>
    <div class="<%= body_class %>">
      <p>
        That is expected, once. Waterhole now also asks for permission to
        <strong>search</strong>: it uses it to have your server fetch a page
        from Waterhole, which your server signs with a key only it holds. That
        signature is how Waterhole tells your server apart from anyone else
        who might later get your domain. Waterhole does not search anything
        else with it.
      </p>
      <p>Approve it as before; after that, signing in works as it always did.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="listed-twice" class="<%= section_class %>">
    <h2 class="<%= heading_class %>">Waterhole is listed twice among my authorized apps</h2>
    <div class="<%= body_class %>">
      <p>
        Asking for the new permission meant registering Waterhole with your
        server again, and Mastodon keeps the old authorization until you
        revoke it. Waterhole no longer uses the older entry, so revoking it
        changes nothing here. In Mastodon it is under
        <% authorized_apps = tag.strong("Preferences → Account → Authorized apps") %>
        <% if signed_in? %>
          <%= link_to authorized_apps, current_instance.authorized_apps_url,
                class: link_class, rel: "noopener", target: "_blank" %>.
        <% else %>
          <%= authorized_apps %>.
        <% end %>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="could-not-prove" class="<%= section_class %>">
    <h2 class="<%= heading_class %>">“… could not prove it is the server that signed up here”</h2>
    <div class="<%= body_class %>">
      <p>
        Your server was asked to fetch a page from Waterhole and sign it, and
        no correctly signed fetch arrived. Nothing you did causes this, and
        trying again rarely helps. The usual causes:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
        <li>Your server cannot reach this Waterhole: a firewall on its outgoing traffic, or this Waterhole's domain blocked on your server.</li>
        <li>Something in front of this Waterhole changes the request on the way, so the signature no longer matches.</li>
        <li>Your domain now points to a different Mastodon server than the one that signed up. Then this is the check doing its job.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        An administrator of your instance can check the first and the last;
        the operator of this Waterhole the second
        (<%= link_to "contact details", imprint_path, class: link_class %>).
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="no-instance-actor" class="<%= section_class %>">
    <h2 class="<%= heading_class %>">“Could not reach … /actor …”</h2>
    <div class="<%= body_class %>">
      <p>
        Waterhole reads your server's public key from its instance actor, at
        <code>/actor</code> on your domain, before sending you to sign in.
        Every Mastodon server publishes one. If it could not be read, the
        server was unreachable or something other than Mastodon answered;
        an administrator of your instance can check what the address shows.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>
